}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.75).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.75) & ≈P(Z<\\frac{1.75-1.5}{\\sqrt{0.0125}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<2.24) \\\\  & =Φ(2.24).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(2.24)=0.9875.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.75) & =Φ(2.24) \\\\  & =0.9875.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534859 17592186045417 true] [17592186441383 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441380 13194139534859 17592186045417 true] [17592186441383 1069 "q-143504" 13194139534859 17592186045417 true] [17592186441383 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534859 17592186045417 true] [17592186441383 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{25}$ be a random sample of size $n=25$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}, & 1\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.75,$ calculate $P(2.4<\\overset{―}{X}<2.7).$\n\n*Hint: Assume that the sample size is sufficiently large for the central limit theorem to be applied. Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.09\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2514\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2483\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2451\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.5\\,$</th><td align=\"ce