ned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{3}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.75.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(2.5,\\frac{0.75}{25}) \\\\  & ∼N(2.5,0.03).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(2.4<\\overset{―}{X}<2.7).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2.4<\\overset{―}{X}<2.7) & =P(\\frac{2.4-2.5}{\\sqrt{0.03}}<Z<\\frac{2.7-2.5}{\\sqrt{0.03}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.58<Z<1.15) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.15)-P(Z<-0.58) \\\\  & =Φ(1.15)-Φ(-0.58)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.15) & =0.8749, \\\\ Φ(-0.58) & =0.2810.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2.4<\\overset{―}{X}<2.7) & =Φ(1.15)-Φ(-0.58) \\\\  & =0.8749-0.2810 \\\\  & =0.5939.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534859 17592186045417 true] [17592186441384 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441379 13194139534859 17592186045417 true] [17592186441384 1069 "q-143505" 13194139534859 17592186045417 true] [17592186441384 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534859 17592186045417 true] [17592186441384 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{20}$ be a random sample of size $n=20$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.04,$ calculate $P(\\overset{―}{X}<0.8).$\n\n*Hint: Assume that the sample size is sufficiently large for the central limit theorem to be applied. A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumul