{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.4+2\\cdot 0.35+4\\cdot 0.1+10\\cdot 0.15 \\\\  & =0.4+0.7+0.4+1.5 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =({1}^{2}\\cdot 0.4+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.35+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{10}^{2}\\cdot 0.15)-{3}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.4+1.4+1.6+15)-9 \\\\  & =9.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(3,\\frac{9.4}{47}) \\\\  & ∼N(3,0.2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>3.2).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>3.2) & ≈P(Z>\\frac{3.2-3}{\\sqrt{0.2}}) \\\\  & =P(Z>0.45) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.45) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.45).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.45)=0.6736.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>3.2) & =1-Φ(0.45) \\\\  & =1-0.6736 \\\\  & =0.3264.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534859 17592186045417 true] [17592186441386 1062 #uuid "49fa5f16-09ac-5d8b-8b1c-9bcf22a6ee5d" 13194139534859 17592186045417 true] [17592186441386 1063 #edb/ref 17592186441382 13194139534859 17592186045417 true] [17592186441387 1058 #edb/ref 17592186441390 13194139534859 17592186045417 true] [17592186441387 1062 #uuid "553dca13-5fb8-5232-a65f-f8f4dfa30bae" 13194139534859 17592186045417 true] [17592186441387 1063 #edb/ref 17592186441384 13194139534859 17592186045417 true] [17592186441388 1058 #edb/ref 17592186441387 13194139534859 17592186045417 true] [17592186441388 1062 #uuid "852d10ee-a259-5496-9a87-055b498422f9" 13194139534859 17592186045417 true] [17592186441388 1063 #edb/ref 17592186441383 13194139534859 17592186045417 true] [17592186441389 1058 #edb/ref 17592186441388 13194139534859 17592186045417 true]